how to get clients to pay so I got a  question from our YouTube viewers Jason  love you guys  Jason wrote in to say Evan have you made  a video about how to get people to pay  up when they owe you money I have some  invoices hitting 90 days and I'm trying  to get the money and still keep the  business any thoughts  so Jason this is a interesting and  important question part of this okay  before we even looking at the clients  who owe you money part of this is one  you starting to work with better clients  and doing a better job up front and  finding and work with clients who are  gonna be your ideal clients okay and I  know it when you're first starting a  relationship with a client everybody's  happy everybody's excited you're all  ready to go and then problems can arise  and so doing a better job upfront of  classifying and qualifying your clients  will help you so you don't get to the  situation where you're 90 days and you  haven't been paid yet the other thing to  consider again when working with clients  your next time around is getting more  money up front I don't know what you're  selling I don't know what your payment  schedule looks like but you shouldn't  just be collecting all the money at the  end get at least half of it upfront so  you have some cash flow coming in you  know that the client is committed to the  project right if they're not willing to  give you 50% up front and they want  everything back loaded that should set  off some warning signs for you to say  hey maybe this client is not going to be  a great client for me that won't help  you in your situation right now but will  help you with your future clients okay  and a lot of entrepreneurs have had to  make similar pricing policy changes in  their business because they've been  burned by other clients so nobody  typically sets out and is proactive  about this we've set our policies  because we've been burned in the past by  other clients now what do you do so  you're getting the 90 days or your past  90 days you're following up you're not  getting a response what I would do is  probably try to reach out one last time  with a personal appeal and let them know  how important this is to your business  when I'm working  with smaller businesses I always try to  pay before they ask for because I know  how important it is for a small company  I don't negotiate terms with a small  company and I'm dealing with a huge  company a bank or a telco or or company  who I know doesn't really care about me  I'll negotiate as hard as I can but for  a small company  I usually don't negotiate that much and  so I just let them know how important  this is to you how it impacts your  business that you haven't been paid yet  and then from there if they still don't  respond if it were me I would honestly  probably just let it go I would let it  go because I hate having hassles in my  business and it's not just the time of  following up continuously with them it's  the negative energy right I think about  it when you're following up when you're  thinking about that invoice that's due  and they're your own ex thousand dollars  how does it make you feel not great  right every time you think about that  invoice every time we think about  calling the client which may be every  day now it doesn't make you feel good  and that negativity flows into your  business it flows into your personal  life nothing is good and I would just if  it were me I would just avoid it I just  let the client go and let that you know  that money that I lost be an educational  lesson for me that I won't make that  same mistake again on future clients if  you don't want to let it go okay the  money is really important to you or you  just can't let it go for whatever reason  your next step would be to get a lawyer  to write a letter so you can get a  lawyer either it's a friend of yours who  won't charge you or lawyers just for  half an hour we'll sit down and write a  letter and this shows that you are  serious okay now it's it's going beyond  just a personal appeal and now you're  not quite threatening to sue them yet  but you know you're you're you're making  it known that it's more important that  you might get lawyers involved if they  don't pay up at this point you're  risking losing the relationship but  again I don't know how much you want to  keep a client who often 90 days is not  paying you right this is not an ideal  client for you it's better to focus on  the better ones if they don't pay up if  you don't hear back your next step up is  to either take them to court and  depending on how much you're asking for  or you might qualify to go to the small  claims court or you could sell your  receivables and here you're selling it  to a company who's going to then hound  this client to pay up and you get very  little money you might get 10 cents on  the dollar but you know you have a  little bit of cash and that receivables  off your books but don't count on that  customer ever coming back  so that's what I would do I know it's a  tough situation and and hopefully you  can use it as an educational tool so  that your future clients you don't make  the same mistake and you start working  with better clients we've I think all at  some point been burned by clients who  don't pay us and we become stronger for  it  so hopefully you can get through this  and still have your business and still  do great things with your future believe  for those you watching like the video  thumbs up below let me know what you  think let me know how you handle clients  who don't pay up leave a comment  underneath you have a question for me  leave it below and you can click on my  face to subscribe to the channel to see  more videos like this thank you so much  I'll see you soon
 

